# Lore for the in-game People window, part 2.
LORE = {
'akan': ("The Akan built the forest kingdoms of the gold coast, above all the Asante empire, founded around 1700 under Osei Tutu and the Golden Stool, which fought the British in a century of wars before Ghana became the first African colony to win independence, in 1957.",
         "They weave kente cloth, honour the chiefs and queen mothers at durbars, carve Adinkra symbols with proverbs in them, eat fufu with light soup, and dance to highlife music."),
'gan': ("The Gan people of Jiangxi have lived along the Gan river and Poyang Lake since the Han migrations; Jingdezhen in their province made the porcelain of China's emperors for a thousand years, and their hills were a cradle of the Chinese revolution.",
        "They are known for porcelain and tea, spicy rice noodles, the Gan opera and pilgrimages to the Taoist and Buddhist mountains of Lushan and Longhu."),
'uyghur': ("The Uyghur Khaganate ruled the Mongolian steppe in the eighth and ninth centuries; Uyghurs then settled the oasis cities of the Tarim basin on the Silk Road, became Muslim, and lived under Chinese rule from the eighteenth century.",
           "Their muqam music is a world heritage of song and dance, they bake nan in clay ovens, eat polo and laghman noodles, and their bazaars in Kashgar and Turpan grapes are famous."),
'pahari': ("The Pahari peoples of the western Himalaya lived in small hill kingdoms, Garhwal, Kumaon and the Himachal states, for centuries; the British built hill stations like Shimla and Mussoorie in their mountains.",
           "They keep village deities carried in palanquins to fairs, worship at the Char Dham pilgrimage shrines, weave wool shawls, and dance the nati in long lines in the snow country."),
'uzbek': ("The Uzbeks take their name from a Golden Horde khan; their land is the heart of the Silk Road, Samarkand, Bukhara and Khiva, where Timur ruled and Ulugh Beg built his observatory.",
          "They are famous for plov cooked in great iron pots, nan stamped with patterns, blue-tiled mosques and madrasas, suzani embroidery and the mahalla, the close neighbourhood community."),
'oromo': ("The Oromo are the largest people of the Horn of Africa; they spread across Ethiopia in the sixteenth century, organised by the gadaa system of age grades, and were absorbed into the Ethiopian empire in the nineteenth century.",
          "Their gadaa democracy elects leaders every eight years; they celebrate Irreecha, the thanksgiving at lakes and rivers, and drink buna, coffee, which they say was first found in their land."),
'bedouin': ("The Bedouin are the desert Arab tribes, camel and goat herders of Arabia, Syria, Jordan, the Negev and Sinai; they were the backbone of the Arab conquests and of the Arab revolt of 1916.",
            "They live by hospitality to any traveller, coffee with cardamom roasted by the fire, Nabati poetry, falconry and the black goat-hair tent, though most now live in towns."),
'bulgarian': ("Bulgar Turks and South Slavs founded the First Bulgarian Empire in 681; it adopted Orthodoxy and the Cyrillic alphabet, created there by the disciples of Cyril and Methodius, before five centuries of Ottoman rule ended in 1878.",
              "They celebrate Baba Marta with red and white martenitsa threads, make yoghurt they consider the best in the world, dance the horo, roast peppers in autumn and sing in close harmony."),
'iraqi': ("Mesopotamia between the Tigris and Euphrates was the birthplace of writing and the first cities, Ur, Babylon and Nineveh; Baghdad was the capital of the Abbasid caliphate and the greatest city of the medieval world.",
          "They eat masgouf, fish grilled by the river, kleicha biscuits, and dolma; they love maqam music and poetry, and the Shia pilgrimages to Karbala and Najaf bring millions every year."),
'portuguese': ("Portugal was born in the twelfth century and set its borders early; its navigators opened the sea route to India and Brazil in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, building the first global empire, and the Carnation Revolution of 1974 brought democracy.",
               "They sing fado of longing, saudade, eat bacalhau in a hundred ways, grilled sardines in June for Santo Antonio, and pasteis de nata with strong coffee."),
'mongo': ("The Mongo and other forest peoples live in the great bend of the Congo river, in the heart of the rainforest; the rubber terror of the Congo Free State hit them hardest in the 1890s and 1900s.",
          "They live by fishing, farming and hunting in the forest, travel by pirogue on the rivers, and keep oral epics like Lianja and the ancestors' wisdom in proverb and song."),
'hakka': ("The Hakka, the guest people, migrated from northern China to the southern hills in several waves; they led the Taiping rebellion of the nineteenth century and settled across Southeast Asia, Taiwan and beyond.",
          "They build round earthen tulou fortresses for whole clans, cook salt-baked chicken and stuffed tofu, sing mountain songs, and are famous for thrift, education and women who worked the fields."),
'omani': ("Oman was a great seafaring nation; its sultans ruled Zanzibar and the Swahili coast in the nineteenth century, and its sailors traded from East Africa to China.",
          "They keep frankincense, halwa and coffee for guests, the khanjar dagger, Ibadi Islam with its tradition of moderation, falaj irrigation channels and the dhow building of Sur."),
'burman': ("The Bamar founded the kingdom of Pagan in the ninth century, with its thousands of temples; the Taungoo and Konbaung dynasties built empires before the British conquest in 1885.",
           "They are Theravada Buddhists who send their sons to the monastery, wear thanaka paste on their faces, eat mohinga for breakfast, and celebrate the Thingyan water festival."),
'fang': ("The Fang and the other peoples of Cameroon and Gabon migrated through the equatorial forest; the Fang are famous for the reliquary guardian figures that inspired Picasso and the modern artists.",
         "They play the mvet harp-zither to accompany epics, keep the Bwiti initiation in Gabon, and eat ndole, plantains and cassava, while Cameroon's makossa and bikutsi fill the dancehalls."),
'romanian': ("They speak a Romance language descended from the Latin of Roman Dacia, kept through centuries among Slavs and Hungarians; the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia united in 1859 and Romania was enlarged in 1918.",
             "They keep the painted monasteries of Bukovina, carved wooden churches of Maramures, eat sarmale and mamaliga, dance the hora, and spin folk tales of strigoi and Dracula."),
'czech': ("The Czech lands were the kingdom of Bohemia, seat of the Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV in Prague; the Hussite reformation, the Thirty Years War, and the Velvet Revolution of 1989 marked them.",
          "They brew and drink more beer than anyone, eat svickova and dumplings, love puppets, mushroom hunting and their cottages, and are proud of Dvorak, Kafka and Havel."),
'great_lakes': ("The Great Lakes region of Africa saw the kingdoms of Buganda, Bunyoro, Rwanda, Burundi and others, with kings, drums and bark cloth; colonial rule and the genocide of 1994 left deep wounds.",
                "They grow bananas and coffee on volcanic soil, eat matoke, dance to royal drums, and keep oral histories of their kings."),
'tajik': ("The Tajiks are Persian-speaking heirs of the Samanids, whose capital Bukhara was a centre of the Persian renaissance in the tenth century; they live in the high valleys of the Pamirs and the Fergana basin.",
          "They love the poetry of Rudaki and Rumi, the shashmaqam music, plov and qurutob, and celebrate Navruz with sumalak, a dish stirred all night by the women."),
'ovimbundu': ("The Ovimbundu of the central plateau of Angola were great caravan traders between the coast and the interior; Angola fought a long war of independence against Portugal and a civil war after 1975.",
              "They eat funge and moamba de galinha, dance semba and kizomba, and the music of Luanda and Benguela is famous across the Portuguese-speaking world."),
'zhuang': ("The Zhuang are the largest minority of China, a Tai people of Guangxi who farmed rice on terraces long before Han settlers arrived; their leaders resisted the empire many times.",
           "They hold singing festivals where young people meet by trading improvised songs, play the bronze drums, and weave brocade."),
'welsh': ("The Welsh are descended from the Britons who held the west of the island against the Anglo-Saxons; Llywelyn ap Gruffudd was the last prince of an independent Wales, conquered by England in 1282.",
          "They keep the Welsh language alive, sing in male voice choirs, hold the eisteddfod festivals of poetry and music, and are passionate about rugby."),
'malayali': ("Kerala traded pepper and spices with Romans, Arabs and Chinese for two thousand years; its people include old communities of Syrian Christians, Jews and Muslims beside Hindus.",
             "They perform Kathakali dance-drama, race snake boats on the backwaters during Onam, eat sadya feasts on banana leaves, and have the highest literacy in India."),
'azerbaijani': ("The Azerbaijanis are a Turkic people who settled the lands west of the Caspian among older Caucasian and Iranian peoples; the Safavid dynasty that made Iran Shia came from them, and the north became a Soviet republic and then independent.",
                "They sing mugham, play the tar and kamancha, eat plov with saffron, dolma and kebab, weave carpets, and celebrate Novruz with bonfires and wheat sprouts."),
'saraiki': ("The Saraiki of the south Punjab are heirs of Multan, the city of saints, one of the oldest cities of the subcontinent, and of the Bahawalpur state.",
            "They revere the Sufi poet Khwaja Ghulam Farid, dance the jhumar, wear Ajrak, and grow cotton and mangoes along the Indus."),
'zapotec': ("The Zapotec and Mixtec built Monte Alban and Mitla in the Oaxaca valleys long before the Aztecs; Benito Juarez, the Zapotec president, became Mexico's national hero.",
            "They celebrate the Guelaguetza festival of dance, cook seven moles and tlayudas, make mezcal, weave rugs in Teotitlan and black pottery in Coyotepec."),
'nilotic': ("The Nilotic peoples, Dinka, Nuer, Shilluk and others, spread along the Nile and the Great Lakes; they are cattle herders who lived through slaving raids, colonial rule and the long civil wars of Sudan.",
            "Their cattle are their wealth and poetry, they hold songs for favourite oxen, scarify faces as marks of manhood, and gather in cattle camps by the river."),
'xiang': ("The Xiang of Hunan were the frontier of the Chu kingdom; in modern times Hunan gave China many soldiers and revolutionaries, including Mao Zedong.",
          "They love the hottest food in China, smoked pork and chilli fish heads, and the Xiang embroidery and Hunan opera."),
'belarusian': ("The Belarusians descend from the East Slavs of Polotsk; they lived in the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, then under Russia; the Second World War killed a quarter of them.",
               "They eat draniki potato pancakes, keep Kupalle at midsummer, weave straw crafts and sing folk songs of the forest."),
'shona': ("The Shona built Great Zimbabwe, the stone city that gave its name to the country; the kingdom of Mutapa traded gold with the coast. Zimbabwe won its independence in 1980.",
          "They play the mbira at bira ceremonies to call the ancestors, carve stone sculpture, eat sadza and relish, and love chimurenga music."),
'dutch': ("The Dutch and Flemish built cities on reclaimed land, fought Spain for independence in the Eighty Years War, and in the seventeenth century made the Republic the richest trading nation of Europe.",
          "They cycle everywhere, eat herring, cheese and stroopwafels, celebrate King's Day in orange and Sinterklaas in December, and are proud of Rembrandt and Van Gogh."),
'kanuri': ("The Kanuri ruled the Kanem-Bornu empire around Lake Chad for a thousand years, one of the longest-lived states in history.",
           "They hold durbars with horsemen, eat millet and fish from the lake, and trade across the Sahel, where their language was for centuries a lingua franca."),
'lao': ("The Lao kingdom of Lan Xang, the land of a million elephants, was founded in 1353; later it was divided among Siam and France.",
        "They eat sticky rice with laap, hold the Boun Bang Fai rocket festival, play the khaen mouth organ, and give alms to monks at dawn in Luang Prabang."),
'miao': ("The Miao and Hmong are mountain people of southern China; many rebelled against the Qing, and some migrated to Laos, Vietnam and Thailand, fighting in the Indochina wars.",
         "They are famous for silver headdresses, batik and embroidery, the New Year with courtship games, and the reed pipe lusheng."),
'santal': ("The Santals are among the largest Adivasi peoples of India; they rose in the Santal Hul of 1855 against landlords and the British.",
           "They dance in lines to the tamak and tumdak drums, worship the sacred grove, and write their language in the Ol Chiki script."),
'swiss_romansh': ("The Swiss Alpine peoples, Romansh, German and Italian speakers of the high valleys, kept their freedom in confederation since 1291 and their ancient languages in remote valleys.",
                  "They keep cow processions to the summer pastures, yodel, play the alphorn, eat raclette, fondue and capuns, and hold village democracy in the Landsgemeinde."),
'baloch': ("The Baloch spread across the arid lands of the Iranian plateau and the Makran coast; their Khanate of Kalat lasted until 1955, split between Iran, Pakistan and Afghanistan.",
           "They keep a code of honour and hospitality, sajji roasted lamb, embroidered dress, and the music of the suroz fiddle."),
'bhil': ("The Bhils are one of India's largest tribal peoples, archers of the Aravalli and Vindhya hills who served Rajput kings and resisted British rule.",
         "They paint vivid dot pictures, celebrate Bhagoria with swings and courtship, and worship ancestral spirits and local gods."),
'finnic': ("The Finns lived under Swedish rule for six centuries and as a Russian grand duchy before independence in 1917; they defended it in the Winter War of 1939-40.",
           "They live by the sauna, sisu (grit), the Kalevala epic, berries and mushrooms of the forest, rye bread, and the lake cottage in summer."),
'khmer': ("The Khmer empire ruled from Angkor, the largest city of the medieval world, between the ninth and fifteenth centuries; modern Cambodia lived through the Khmer Rouge genocide of the 1970s.",
          "They keep apsara dance, Theravada Buddhism, amok fish curry, prahok, and the water festival on the Tonle Sap."),
'yi': ("The Yi of the Liangshan mountains kept their own script, priests and caste system; they ruled the Nanzhao kingdom with the Bai.",
       "They celebrate the Torch Festival, wear black capes, and write their ancient books in the Yi script."),
'banda_zande': ("The Zande built kingdoms in the borderlands between the Nile and the Congo; the Banda and Gbaya lived through slave raids from the north.",
                "They are hunters and farmers who play harps and xylophones, carve wood, and keep oral traditions of oracles and magic."),
'haudenosaunee': ("The Haudenosaunee, the Six Nations, formed the Great Law of Peace centuries ago, a confederacy that some say inspired the US constitution.",
                  "They play lacrosse, which they invented, keep longhouse ceremonies, the clan mothers who choose chiefs, and the wampum belts."),
'shan': ("The Shan are a Tai people of the hills of Burma; their states were ruled by saopha princes until 1962.",
         "They are Buddhists who hold the Poy Sang Long ordination with boys dressed as princes, and eat sticky rice and fermented soybean."),
'guarani_ind': ("The Guarani peoples once lived across much of South America; the Jesuit missions organised them and they fought the Guarani War of 1756.",
                "They seek the Land Without Evil in their prophetic religion, speak Guarani, and grow manioc and maize in the forest."),
'georgian': ("Georgia became Christian in the fourth century; under Queen Tamar it had a golden age, and it lived under Persia, Ottomans and Russia before independence in 1991.",
             "They feast at the supra with a toastmaster, polyphonic songs, khachapuri, khinkali and wine fermented in clay qvevri."),
'catalan': ("Catalonia grew from the Carolingian Marca Hispanica; the Crown of Aragon made it a Mediterranean power, and it lost its institutions in 1714.",
            "They build human towers, castells, dance the sardana, celebrate Sant Jordi with roses and books, and eat pa amb tomaquet."),
'mossi': ("The Mossi kingdoms of Ouagadougou and Yatenga, founded by horsemen in the fifteenth century, resisted the empires of Mali and Songhai.",
          "The Mogho Naba still holds court on Fridays; they eat to, millet porridge, and their masks and festivals mark the farming year."),
'acehnese': ("The Sultanate of Aceh was a powerful trading state and centre of Islamic learning; it fought the Dutch for thirty years.",
             "They dance the Saman with clapping and singing, drink strong Aceh coffee, and keep sharia under special autonomy."),
'armenian': ("Armenia became the first Christian nation in 301, invented its alphabet in 405, and lived under many empires; the genocide of 1915 killed a million and scattered the survivors worldwide.",
             "They keep the Armenian Apostolic Church, the duduk, lavash, khorovats, apricots, and a diaspora of millions."),
'kikuyu': ("The Kikuyu farmed the highlands around Mount Kenya; their land was taken by settlers, and the Mau Mau rising of the 1950s led to Kenya's independence in 1963.",
           "They are farmers and traders, eat githeri and irio, and the circumcision and age-set ceremonies still mark their lives."),
'afro_colombian': ("Afro-Colombians descend from Africans enslaved in the gold mines and plantations; Palenque de San Basilio was the first free town of escaped slaves in the Americas.",
                   "They play the marimba de chonta of the Pacific coast, dance currulao and champeta, and speak Palenquero, a Spanish-based creole."),
'yao_mien': ("The Yao or Mien are mountain people of southern China who migrated to Vietnam, Laos and Thailand; they practise a Taoist religion with their own scriptures.",
             "They wear embroidered clothes and red turbans, hold coming-of-age ceremonies, and keep Taoist rites in Chinese characters."),
'ngalop': ("The Ngalop are the Buddhist people of western Bhutan, united by the Zhabdrung in the seventeenth century; Bhutan never fell to a colonial power.",
           "They wear the gho and kira, hold tshechu festivals with masked dances, play archery, eat ema datshi, chillies with cheese, and measure Gross National Happiness."),
'pa_dutch': ("The Pennsylvania Dutch are descendants of German-speaking Anabaptists and others who settled Pennsylvania from 1683; the Amish and Mennonites among them kept a plain way of life.",
             "They farm with horses, build barns together, make quilts and shoofly pie, and speak Pennsylvania German."),
'naga': ("The Nagas are many tribes of the hills between India and Burma, once headhunters, who fought for independence after 1947.",
         "They hold the Hornbill festival, weave shawls with tribal patterns, build morungs (dormitories), and eat smoked pork with bamboo shoots."),
'occitan': ("Occitania was the land of the troubadours and of the Cathars, crushed by the Albigensian crusade in the thirteenth century.",
            "They speak the langue d'oc, cook cassoulet, play rugby, and remember the troubadours who invented courtly love."),
'manchu': ("The Manchus founded the Qing dynasty, which ruled China from 1644 to 1912 and doubled its size; they became largely Chinese-speaking.",
           "They keep shamanic rites, the qipao dress came from them, and their language survives among the Xibe and in old archives."),
'jamaican': ("Jamaica was a British sugar colony where enslaved Africans outnumbered whites; the Maroons fought the British to a treaty, and Jamaica became independent in 1962.",
             "They gave the world reggae, ska and dancehall, Bob Marley, Rastafari, jerk chicken, ackee and saltfish, and sprinters like Usain Bolt."),
'hungarian': ("The Magyars rode into the Carpathian basin in 895; Saint Stephen founded the kingdom in 1000, Hungary fought the Ottomans and Habsburgs, and lost two thirds of its land at Trianon in 1920.",
              "They cook goulash and paprika chicken, bathe in thermal springs, dance the csardas, and are proud of Liszt and Bartok."),
'gond': ("The Gond and other Adivasi peoples of central India had their own kingdoms in Garha-Mandla and Chanda before the Marathas and British.",
         "They paint Gond art of trees and animals, worship the forest, and dance the Saila and Karma."),
'tujia': ("The Tujia of the Wuling mountains were ruled by native chieftains for centuries before the Qing.",
          "They weave xilankapu brocade, dance the hand-waving dance, and sing crying songs before weddings."),
'ob_exiles': ("The Ob Sea exiles are a people born in the twenty-second century when the thaw and floods of western Siberia created a great inland sea around the Ob and displaced millions.",
              "They live in floating and stilt towns, fish the new sea, keep memorials for drowned villages, and speak a Russian full of words from the many peoples among them."),
'merina': ("The Merina kingdom of the highlands united most of Madagascar in the nineteenth century under King Andrianampoinimerina and Radama, before French conquest in 1895.",
           "They terrace rice fields, wear the lamba shawl, honour the ancestors, and their capital Antananarivo is built on the hills of the old kingdom."),
'afro_carib': ("The Lesser Antilleans and Bahamians descend from Africans brought to the sugar islands; they won emancipation in 1834-38 and independence in the 1960s-80s.",
               "They celebrate Carnival with calypso and soca, Junkanoo in the Bahamas, play cricket, and eat flying fish and cou-cou."),
'navajo': ("The Navajo and Pueblo peoples have lived in the Southwest for centuries; the Pueblo Revolt of 1680 expelled the Spanish, and the Navajo survived the Long Walk of 1864.",
           "They weave rugs and blankets, make silver and turquoise jewellery, keep the kachina dances and the sand paintings, and live by hozho, the balance of beauty."),
'xhosa': ("The Xhosa fought nine frontier wars against the Cape colony; the cattle-killing of 1856-57 was a catastrophe; Nelson Mandela was a Xhosa.",
          "They speak a language with click sounds, keep the initiation of young men, and their beadwork and umngqusho dish are famous."),
'kyrgyz': ("The Kyrgyz are mountain nomads whose epic Manas is one of the longest in the world; they came to the Tian Shan from Siberia centuries ago.",
           "They live in yurts in summer pastures, play kok-boru (a horse game with a goat), hunt with eagles, and drink kumis."),
'austral': ("The Austral settlers came to the ice-free coasts of Antarctica in the twenty-second century, under the Antarctic Trust, from every continent.",
            "They live in sheltered towns among the new tundra, keep midwinter festivals, and care fiercely for the fragile land they inherited."),
'tuareg': ("The Tuareg are Berber nomads of the Sahara; they controlled the caravan routes and resisted French rule, and rose in rebellion several times.",
           "The men wear the indigo veil, they write in Tifinagh, play the imzad fiddle and desert blues guitar, and drink three glasses of tea."),
'igbo': ("The Igbo lived in self-governing villages with a famous tradition of enterprise; the Nri kingdom, the Biafran war of 1967-70, and the novels of Chinua Achebe mark their history.",
         "They celebrate the New Yam festival, masquerades, ofe onugbu soup, and are known as traders across Nigeria."),
'otomi_totonac': ("The Otomi and Totonac are ancient peoples of central Mexico; the Totonacs of Cempoala allied with Cortes against the Aztecs.",
                  "The Totonacs perform the voladores ritual, flying from a high pole, grow vanilla, and the Otomi embroider bright cloths."),
'sami': ("The Sami are the indigenous people of the northern Nordic countries and the Kola peninsula; they were pressured by the states to give up their language and land.",
         "They herd reindeer, sing the yoik, wear the colourful gakti, and hold the Easter festivals of Kautokeino."),
'tsonga': ("The Tsonga live in southern Mozambique and South Africa; the Gaza empire of the Nguni ruled them in the nineteenth century.",
           "They dance xibelani with swirling skirts, and their music is famous in South Africa."),
'mizo_chin': ("The Mizo and Chin live in the hills between India and Burma; they became largely Christian under missionaries.",
              "They dance cheraw with bamboo poles, weave puan cloth, and sing hymns in great choirs."),
'galician': ("Galicia was a Celtic and Suevi land, then a kingdom in the Middle Ages; Santiago de Compostela became the great pilgrimage of Europe.",
             "They play the bagpipe gaita, eat octopus and empanada, drink Albarino, and speak Galician, close to Portuguese."),
'gilaki': ("The Gilaks and Mazandaranis live on the green Caspian coast of Iran, behind the Alborz mountains; their land resisted the Arab conquest longest.",
           "They grow rice and tea, eat mirza ghasemi and fish, and keep their own languages."),
'buryat': ("The Buryats are Mongol people around Lake Baikal; Russia absorbed them in the seventeenth century.",
           "They keep Buddhism and shamanism, celebrate Sagaalgan, eat buuza, and revere Baikal as sacred."),
'ncaucasian': ("The North Caucasians are dozens of peoples in the mountains who fought the Russian conquest for decades in the nineteenth century.",
               "They dance the lezginka, keep codes of honour and hospitality, and make khinkal and cheese."),
'bhojpuri': ("The Bhojpuris of Bihar and eastern Uttar Pradesh are heirs of ancient Magadha; many emigrated as indentured labourers to the Caribbean, Mauritius and Fiji.",
             "They celebrate Chhath with offerings to the sun at rivers, eat litti chokha, and love Bhojpuri folk songs and films."),
'purepecha': ("The Purepecha empire of Michoacan resisted the Aztecs and was never conquered by them.",
              "They make copper, lacquer and pottery, dance the Dance of the Old Men, and hold the Night of the Dead on Lake Patzcuaro."),
'timorese': ("The peoples of Timor, Flores and the Moluccas are Austronesian and Papuan; the Moluccas were the spice islands, and East Timor won independence in 2002.",
             "They weave ikat cloth, build sacred houses, and keep Catholic and ancestral traditions."),
'jewish': ("The Jews are an ancient people of the land of Israel; after two thousand years of diaspora and the Holocaust, they founded the state of Israel in 1948.",
           "They keep Shabbat, Passover and Yom Kippur, speak Hebrew revived as a living language, and blend cooking from every diaspora."),
'albanian': ("The Albanians may descend from the ancient Illyrians; Skanderbeg fought the Ottomans in the fifteenth century, and Albania became independent in 1912.",
             "They keep besa, the word of honour, sing iso-polyphony, and celebrate Flag Day."),
'bodo': ("The Bodos are an indigenous people of the Brahmaputra valley who fought for a homeland of their own.",
         "They dance the Bagurumba, weave the dokhona dress, and worship Bathou."),
'mbundu': ("The Mbundu kingdom of Ndongo resisted the Portuguese under Queen Nzinga in the seventeenth century.",
           "Kimbundu is their language; they invented semba music, and Luanda is in their land."),
'lozi_tonga': ("The Lozi kingdom of Barotseland on the Zambezi floodplain and the Tonga of the Zambezi valley.",
               "The Lozi king moves to higher ground in the Kuomboka ceremony, and the Tonga kept their culture through the flooding of Lake Kariba."),
'amhara': ("The Amhara were the ruling people of the Ethiopian empire for centuries; Ethiopia defeated Italy at Adwa in 1896 and was never colonised.",
           "They keep the Orthodox faith, fasting, Timkat and Meskel, eat injera with wot, and dance the eskista with their shoulders."),
'ticuna_shipibo': ("The Ticuna and Shipibo are peoples of the upper Amazon; the Ticuna live where Brazil, Peru and Colombia meet.",
                   "The Shipibo paint kene patterns that are songs made visible, and the Ticuna mask festivals mark girls' coming of age."),
}
